A composable called inside setup gets three things for free:
Every computed, watch and watchEffect it creates is collected by the component's effect scope and stopped on unmount (Post 00, Chapter 7).
Lifecycle hooks it registers (onMounted, onUnmounted) attach to the calling component.
inject() resolves against the calling component's ancestors.
All three depend on the current instance, which Vue sets while setup runs synchronously. That gives the first rule: call composables synchronously at the top of setup, not in event handlers, setTimeout callbacks or after an arbitrary await.
<script setup> softens the await part. The compiler wraps each top-level await so the instance is restored afterwards. This source:
<script setup lang="ts">
const user = await fetchUser()
const route = useRoute()
</script>
compiles (3.5) to:
async setup(__props) {
let __temp: any, __restore: any
const user = (
([__temp, __restore] = _withAsyncContext(() => fetchUser())),
__temp = await __temp,
__restore(),
__temp
)
const route = useRoute()
// …
}
__restore() puts the instance back, so useRoute() after the await still works. A plain async setup() in an export default {} component, or an async function you wrote yourself, gets no such help. The top-level await also turns the component into an async dependency that needs a <Suspense> ancestor (Chapter 8).

// Caller-friendly: destructuring keeps reactivity
export function useCounter() {
const count = ref(0)
const double = computed(() => count.value * 2)
const inc = () => count.value++
return { count: readonly(count), double, inc }
}
const { count, inc } = useCounter() // count is still a ref
Returning reactive({ count, double }) would make every caller that destructures lose reactivity (Post 00, Chapter 8). If a caller wants the object form, it can wrap the result itself: const counter = reactive(useCounter()) unwraps the refs.
Trick: return readonly(ref) for state the composable owns and a function for each allowed mutation. Callers cannot bypass your invariants, and a code search for inc( finds every writer.

A composable that takes a string snapshot freezes at its first value. One that accepts only Ref<string> forces callers to wrap props and computed values. Accept all three:
import { shallowRef, toValue, watchEffect, onWatcherCleanup, readonly, type MaybeRefOrGetter } from 'vue'
export function useJson<T>(url: MaybeRefOrGetter<string | null>) {
const data = shallowRef<T>()
const error = shallowRef<Error>()
const pending = shallowRef(false)
watchEffect(async () => {
const u = toValue(url) // read INSIDE the effect, before any await: tracked
if (!u) return
const ctrl = new AbortController()
onWatcherCleanup(() => ctrl.abort())
pending.value = true
error.value = undefined
try {
const res = await fetch(u, { signal: ctrl.signal })
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`)
data.value = (await res.json()) as T
} catch (e) {
if ((e as Error).name !== 'AbortError') error.value = e as Error
} finally {
if (!ctrl.signal.aborted) pending.value = false
}
})
return { data: readonly(data), error: readonly(error), pending: readonly(pending) }
}
Callers can now pass anything:
const props = defineProps<{ id: number }>()
useJson(`/api/users/1`) // plain value: fetched once
useJson(urlRef) // ref
useJson(() => `/api/users/${props.id}`) // getter over a prop: refetches when id changes
Three details in that composable are deliberate:
toValue(url) runs inside watchEffect and before the first await, so the dependency is tracked (Post 00, Chapter 5).
onWatcherCleanup aborts the previous request when url changes and when the component unmounts, so a slow old response can never overwrite a newer one.
shallowRef for data: API responses are replaced, not mutated, and a deep proxy over a large JSON tree is wasted work. Nuxt 4 made the same choice for useFetch (Post 03).

export function useEventListener<K extends keyof WindowEventMap>(
type: K, handler: (e: WindowEventMap[K]) => void, opts?: AddEventListenerOptions
) {
window.addEventListener(type, handler, opts)
const stop = () => window.removeEventListener(type, handler, opts)
if (getCurrentScope()) onScopeDispose(stop)
return stop
}
onScopeDispose runs when the component unmounts and when a standalone effectScope stops, so the composable also works in a store, a test, or another composable's scope. The getCurrentScope() guard avoids a development warning when the function is called outside any scope; returning stop gives such callers a way to clean up by hand.
Watch out: window does not exist during SSR. A composable that touches browser APIs at call time crashes the server render. Do browser work in onMounted (which never runs on the server) or guard with typeof window !== 'undefined'.

Some state should exist once for all components that use it: one resize listener, one WebSocket, one media-query watcher. A detached scope owns it, and reference counting disposes it when the last user unmounts:
import { effectScope, onScopeDispose, getCurrentScope, type EffectScope } from 'vue'
export function createSharedComposable<T>(factory: () => T): () => T {
let users = 0
let state: T | undefined
let scope: EffectScope | undefined
const release = () => {
if (--users <= 0 && scope) {
scope.stop()
scope = state = undefined
users = 0
}
}
return () => {
users++
if (!scope) {
scope = effectScope(true) // detached: not collected by the first caller's scope
state = scope.run(factory)
}
if (getCurrentScope()) onScopeDispose(release)
return state as T
}
}
export const useWindowWidth = createSharedComposable(() => {
const width = shallowRef(window.innerWidth)
useEventListener('resize', () => (width.value = window.innerWidth), { passive: true })
return readonly(width)
})
Without effectScope(true), the listener and any watchers would be collected by the first component that called useWindowWidth, and would stop when that component unmounted even though others still use them. VueUse ships a production version of this helper under the same name.

// cart-context.ts
import type { InjectionKey, Ref } from 'vue'
export interface CartContext {
items: Readonly<Ref<readonly CartItem[]>>
add(item: CartItem): void
remove(id: string): void
}
export const CartKey: InjectionKey<CartContext> = Symbol('cart')
// CartProvider.vue <script setup>
const items = ref<CartItem[]>([])
provide(CartKey, {
items: readonly(items),
add: (item) => items.value.push(item),
remove: (id) => { items.value = items.value.filter((i) => i.id !== id) }
})
inject(CartKey) is now typed as CartContext | undefined. The provider exposes read-only state and named mutations, which keeps writes in one place, the same shape as the composable return in Chapter 1.

undefined checks at every call site are noise. Wrap the pair once:
export function useCart(): CartContext {
const ctx = inject(CartKey)
if (!ctx) throw new Error('useCart() needs a <CartProvider> ancestor')
return ctx
}

Since 3.5, destructuring defineProps() is stable and enabled by default, with native default values:
<script setup lang="ts">
import { computed, watch } from 'vue'
const { count = 0, label } = defineProps<{ count?: number; label: string }>()
const double = computed(() => count * 2)
watch(() => count, (v) => console.log(v))
</script>
The compiler rewrites every use of a destructured name into a property read on the props proxy. The relevant part of the 3.5 output:
props: {
count: { type: Number, required: false, default: 0 },
label: { type: String, required: true }
},
setup(__props: any) {
const double = computed(() => __props.count * 2)
watch(() => __props.count, (v) => console.log(v))
}
So count is not a local variable at run time; it is __props.count, read inside the computed getter and tracked. Two consequences:
Passing the name itself to watch(count, …) would pass a number. The compiler rejects it: "count" is a destructured prop and should not be passed directly to watch(). Pass a getter () => count instead.
Passing it to a composable passes a snapshot. Use useThing(() => count); with MaybeRefOrGetter inputs (Chapter 2) this is free.
withDefaults(defineProps<…>(), { … }) still works and remains the way to declare defaults without destructuring.

defineModel declares a prop and its update event in one line and returns a ref that reads the prop and emits on write:
const model = defineModel<string>({ required: true }) // v-model
const [title, titleModifiers] = defineModel<string, 'trim'>('title') // v-model:title with modifiers
The 3.5 compiler turns this into a modelValue prop, a modelModifiers prop, a title prop, a titleModifiers prop, the events update:modelValue and update:title, and two useModel(__props, …) calls. Modifiers can be applied in a set transformer:
const [value, modifiers] = defineModel<string, 'uppercase'>({
set(v) {
return modifiers.uppercase ? v.toUpperCase() : v
}
})
Watch out: a default on defineModel is used when the parent does not bind v-model. The child then works as local state while the parent's own value (if it has one but did not bind it) stays different. The Vue docs warn about this desynchronisation. Give a default only to models that are genuinely optional.

const Chart = defineAsyncComponent({
loader: () => import('./Chart.vue'),
loadingComponent: ChartSkeleton,
delay: 200, // default: show the loading component only after 200 ms
errorComponent: ChartError,
timeout: 10_000, // default: no timeout
onError(error, retry, fail, attempts) {
attempts <= 3 ? retry() : fail()
}
})
The loader becomes a separate chunk. The 200 ms delay exists so fast loads do not flash a skeleton. If the async component is inside a <Suspense>, it is controlled by that boundary by default (suspensible: true) and its own loading, error, delay and timeout options are ignored.

function useTitle(title: string) sets document.title once. Rewrite it so that useTitle('Home'), useTitle(titleRef) and useTitle(() => ${n.value} new) all work and stay in sync.
Solution
export function useTitle(title: MaybeRefOrGetter<string>) {
watchEffect(() => { document.title = toValue(title) })
}
toValue reads inside the effect, so a ref or getter is tracked; a plain string is simply read once. In SSR, move the body into onMounted or use Unhead / Nuxt's useHead.

Write an InjectionKey and provide/use helpers for a Toaster service with show(message: string, tone?: 'info' | 'error'), such that a missing provider throws with a clear message.
Solution
export interface Toaster { show(message: string, tone?: 'info' | 'error'): void }
export const ToasterKey: InjectionKey<Toaster> = Symbol('toaster')
export const provideToaster = (t: Toaster) => provide(ToasterKey, t)
export function useToaster(): Toaster {
const t = inject(ToasterKey)
if (!t) throw new Error('useToaster() needs provideToaster() in an ancestor')
return t
}
